{
  "dom_id": "step-q4062",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$3\\sqrt{18b}+3\\sqrt{12b}+2\\sqrt{27b}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{18b}+3\\sqrt{12b}+2\\sqrt{27b} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{9\\cdot 2b}+3\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3b}+2\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3b} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{2b}+3\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3b}+2\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2b}+3\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3b}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot \\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+6\\sqrt{3b}+6\\sqrt{3b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\sqrt{2b}+6\\sqrt{3b}+6\\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+(6+6)\\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+12\\sqrt{3b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
